{"band":2,"locale":"en","words":[["a sofa","Sit on the sofa and rest.",null],["to walk","I walk in the park.","A ㄷ-irregular verb, like 듣다: 걸어요 and 걸었어요 before a vowel, 걷고 and 걷습니다 before a consonant. 걸어요 is also how 걸다 (to hang, to make a call) conjugates, so 전화를 걸어요 is making a call and 공원을 걸어요 is walking in the park — the object decides. Note the noun 걸음, a step."],["that time, then","It was raining then.",null],["to study","I study Korean every day.",null],["a message","I received a message.",null],["a young animal","The kitten is cute.","The young of an animal — a kitten is 고양이 새끼. Said of a person it is a serious insult, and it is the second half of some of the coarsest words in Korean. Keep it for animals."],["a match, a game","The match starts soon.",null],["a man's older sister","My older sister is singing.","A man's word for an older sister. A woman would say 언니 for the same person. The pair 누나/언니 works exactly like 형/오빠, one syllable of the family tree carried by the speaker."],["doll","The child hugs a doll.",null],["a bottle","I threw the empty bottle out.","A bottle. The same syllable is also the word for illness, so 병이 있어요 can be there is a bottle or there is a disease, and 병에 걸렸어요 — caught a 병 — is always the illness one."],["a drinking glass","Just one glass of water, please.",null],["situation","The situation is not good.",null],["Saturday","Let's meet on Saturday.",null],["to be sad","The story is sad.",null],["roof","The roof is red.",null],["nearby","There is a shop nearby.",null],["to not work out","My studying isn't going well.",null],["a scene","I still remember that scene.",null],["to prepare","I'm preparing for the exam.",null],["so, so much","Please don't do that.",null],["chopsticks","I pick up a side dish with chopsticks.",null],["a courtroom","I have to go to the courtroom tomorrow.",null],["toy, plaything","There are many toys.",null],["to be expensive","These clothes are expensive.",null],["that one","What is that over there?",null],["to close one's eyes","Please close your eyes.",null],["a smell","There's a nice smell.",null],["a competition","I entered a singing competition.",null],["to be fast","The train is fast.","A 르-irregular adjective like 다르다: 빨라요 and 빨랐어요 before 아/어, but 빠르고, 빠릅니다 and 빠른 기차 (a fast train) are regular. The adverb is 빨리, quickly — 빨리 오세요, come quickly — and 빨리빨리 is the well-known Korean word for hurry. Its opposite is 느리다."],["danger","The risk of an accident is high.",null],["free of charge","Entry is free.",null],["to pass by","Time passes quickly.",null],["to be happy","I am happy right now.",null],["a tunnel","The train went through the tunnel.",null],["someone's words","I listened to the teacher's words.",null],["to believe, to trust","I trust my friend.",null],["a half","I cut the apple in half.","A half. The same syllable is also a class at school — 우리 반, our class — and it is how you say half past: 두 시 반 is half past two."],["to put into a container","I put rice in the bowl.",null],["an invitation","I received an invitation.",null],["a factory","I work in a factory.",null],["feeling","I have a good feeling.",null],["the most, number one","This one is the best.",null],["smoke","Smoke is coming out.",null],["a duck","The duck plays in the water.",null],["surgery","The operation went well.",null],["a fist","I clenched my fist tightly.",null],["a wheel","The wheel rolls.",null],["a desert","The desert is very hot in the daytime.",null],["not at all","I had no idea at all.",null],["a guest, a customer","A guest has arrived.",null],["this place","I like this place.",null],["an exit","The exit is over there.",null],["a lie","Please don't lie.",null],["the forehead","Sweat came out on my forehead.",null],["sand","There is a lot of sand at the sea.",null],["quite a while","I waited quite a while.",null],["the colour red","The red apple looks delicious.",null],["baseball","I watch baseball.",null],["to invite","I invited a friend home.",null],["a tooth","The puppy lost a tooth.",null],["a phone call","The line is busy.",null],["always","You are always late.",null],["a whale","Whales are very big.",null],["mask","I put on a mask.",null],["a decision","I made a decision.",null],["an incident, a case","There was a big incident.",null],["popularity","This song is very popular.",null],["a team","We work together on a team.",null],["oneself","Believe in yourself.",null],["a score","The score went up a lot this time.",null],["instant noodles","I cooked instant noodles at night.",null],["right away, immediately","I'll go right away.",null],["the chin, the jaw","I sat with my chin in my hand.",null],["an advertisement","I saw the advertisement.",null],["a middle-aged man","Excuse me, sir!","A man of about your parents' age. Fine when you mean a man you do not know; risky as an address, because a man who does not consider himself middle-aged may take it badly. 저기요 works for anyone."],["day of the week","What day of the week is it today?",null],["fried chicken","I ordered fried chicken.",null],["Friday","There's an exam on Friday.",null],["a bug","A bug is going past.",null],["often","We keep in touch often.",null],["uncle","My uncle has come.",null],["to be young","You're still young.",null],["an owner","The shop owner is kind.",null],["an injury","The player suffered a serious injury.",null],["to sing","Let's sing together.",null],["to congratulate","Happy birthday to you.",null],["an example","Please give one example.",null],["to leave","I leave Seoul tomorrow.",null],["applause","Please give a round of applause.",null],["to drive","Please drive carefully.",null],["a garden","I planted flowers in the garden.",null],["a chilli pepper","The chilli is very hot.",null],["continuously, on and on","It keeps raining.",null],["to be sorry","I'm sorry for being late.","The more formal of the two ways to apologise: 죄송합니다 to strangers, customers, teachers and elders; 미안해요 or 미안합니다 to friends and equals. Using 미안해요 with someone you should be formal with